  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Atlantic Diving Supply obligated $3.8B in federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Atlantic Diving Supply won 55,860 awards this fiscal year to date, with an average award size of $67.7K. Atlantic Diving Supply obligations are down 36.9% year over year. Historical obligations show variation: FY21 was $2.9B, FY22 was $3.1B, FY23 was $4.6B, FY24 was $4.8B, and FY25 was $6B. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $3.6B. The General Services Administration is the next-largest buyer with $95.2M, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $38.1M and the Department of State at $21.2M. Market concentration appears in a few NAICS categories: Service Establishment Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers accounts for $2.2B and All Other Miscellaneous Manufacturing accounts for $1.1B. Other notable markets include Bare Printed Circuit Board Manufacturing at $106M and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing at $37.2M. This picture shows a FY2026 to date portfolio concentrated in Defense and a small set of high-dollar markets.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Atlantic Diving Supply's top federal buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer, obligating $3.6B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration follows with $95.2M, then the Department of Homeland Security with $38.1M and the Department of State with $21.2M. Smaller buying agencies include the Department of Justice at $19.4M and the Department of Energy at $1.9M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What product markets drive Atlantic Diving Supply obligations?",
        "body": "Service Establishment Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers is the largest market at $2.2B. All Other Miscellaneous Manufacturing contributes $1.1B, with Bare Printed Circuit Board Manufacturing at $106M and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing at $37.2M. Several specialized manufacturing and wholesaling categories make up the remainder of FY2026 to date obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
